<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhou Enlai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhou Enlai was the first premier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, serving from 1949 until his death in 1976, and its foreign minister until 1958. A veteran of the Long March, he represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference of 1954 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of 1955 and helped arrange the rapprochement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1971–72.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai.jpg" alt="Zhou Enlai" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: President (1969-1974 : Nixon). White House Photo Office. 1969-1974; General Services Administration. National Archive... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Premier_Chou_En-Lai_in_the_Great_Hall_of_the_People%27s_Banquet_Hall_-_DPLA_-_a40021a8100507bc7a244420481bf488.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
